## **HSRP (Hot Standby Routing Protocol)**

### **The Problem HSRP Solves:**

**Without HSRP:**
```
Servers have default gateway: 10.0.20.1 (CORE-SW1)

If CORE-SW1 crashes:
- All servers lose connectivity
- Had to manually reconfigure each server's gateway
- This takes HOURS!
```

**With HSRP:**
```
Servers have default gateway: 10.0.20.254 (Virtual IP)

CORE-SW1 and CORE-SW2 both "own" this virtual IP
If CORE-SW1 crashes:
- CORE-SW2 automatically takes over the virtual IP
- Servers don't notice! Still sending to .254!
```

### **How HSRP Elects Active/Standby:**

**Priority System:**
```
CORE-SW1 priority = 110 (Active - higher wins)
CORE-SW2 priority = 100 (Standby)

Preempt = Yes, so if CORE-SW1 recovers, it reclaims active role
```

**Virtual IP Addresses in Our Project:**

| VLAN | Virtual IP | Which Switch Active | Priority on SW1 |
|------|-----------|-------------------|-----------------|
| 10 | 10.0.10.254 | CORE-SW1 | 110 |
| 20 | 10.0.20.254 | CORE-SW1 | 110 |
| 30 | 10.0.30.254 | CORE-SW1 | 110 |
| 40 | 10.0.40.254 | CORE-SW1 | 110 |
| 50 | 10.0.50.254 | CORE-SW1 | 110 |
| 60 | 10.0.60.254 | CORE-SW1 | 110 |
| 70 | 10.0.70.254 | CORE-SW1 | 110 |

### **How Servers Use HSRP:**

```
Actual Configuration on Server:
Gateway: 10.0.20.254  (virtual IP, not a real interface!)
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0

Real Devices:
CORE-SW1 VLAN 20 SVI: 10.0.20.1 (part of HSRP group, advertises .254)
CORE-SW2 VLAN 20 SVI: 10.0.20.1 (part of HSRP group, advertises .254)

When server sends a packet:
1. Looks up next-hop for destination
2. Finds gateway .254 in ARP table
3. Actually sends to CORE-SW1's MAC address (active)
4. CORE-SW1 routes the packet

If CORE-SW1 fails:
1. ARP table entry expires
2. Server broadcasts ARP request for .254
3. CORE-SW2 responds with its MAC address
4. Server now sends to CORE-SW2
5. CORE-SW2 routes the packet

Seconds of interruption = acceptable, no reconfiguration needed!
```

