## **VLANs (Virtual LANs)**

### **The Problem VLANs Solve:**

**Old Way (Pre-VLAN):**
```
Database Department:    Physical Switch 1
Web Department:         Physical Switch 2
Security Department:    Physical Switch 3
Management:             Physical Switch 4

This is expensive, complex, and hard to manage!
```

**New Way (VLAN):**
```
Single Physical Switch
├─ VLAN 10: Management
├─ VLAN 20: Servers
├─ VLAN 30: Database
└─ ... etc

All logical, much cheaper!
```

### **How VLANs Work:**

**VLAN Tagging (802.1Q):**

When a frame travels on a trunk port, it gets tagged:
```
Original Frame: [MAC | IP | Data]

Becomes: [MAC | VLAN ID (4802.1Q) | IP | Data]

Receiving Switch:
"This frame is for VLAN 20, I'll forward it to VLAN 20 ports only"
```

**VLAN Isolation (Broadcast Domain):**

```
VLAN 10 (Management)
├─ Server A
├─ Server B
└─ (can communicate with each other)

VLAN 20 (Database)
├─ Server C
├─ Server D
└─ (can communicate with each other, but NOT with VLAN 10)

If Server A broadcasts, only Server B hears it.
Server C does NOT hear it (security benefit!)
```

### **Our VLANs Explained:**

| VLAN | Name | Purpose | Devices | Security Level |
|------|------|---------|---------|-----------------|
| 10 | MANAGEMENT | Network admin access via SSH | Core/Agg switches | High |
| 20 | SERVERS | Web servers, load balancer | ACC-SW1, ACC-SW2 | High |
| 30 | DATABASE | Database servers | ACC-SW2, ACC-SW3 | Very High |
| 40 | INTERNAL | Infrastructure, support services | Various | Medium |
| 50 | SOC | Security Operations Center | ACC-SW3, ACC-SW5, ACC-SW6 | Very High |
| 60 | SIEM | Monitoring & log aggregation | ACC-SW4, ACC-SW5 | High |
| 70 | STORAGE | NAS, backup systems | ACC-SW3, ACC-SW5 | High |
| 80 | PEERING | Inter-site routing | Core layer | Very High |
| 99 | BLACKHOLE | Unused ports (security isolation) | All switches | None |

### **Key VLAN Concepts:**

**Native VLAN:**
- Frames without VLAN tag belong to native VLAN
- Usually VLAN 1 (default)
- In our project: not critical since we use explicit tagging

**Trunk vs Access Ports:**

**Access Port (Host Facing):**
```
Speed: 100 Mbps or 1 Gbps
Carries: Frames for ONE VLAN only (no tag)
Used for: Connecting end devices (servers, computers)
Example: FastEthernet0/3-24 on access switches

When device sends frame to access port:
- Switch adds VLAN tag
- Internally processes as that VLAN
- Can only forward to ports in that VLAN
```

**Trunk Port (Switch-to-Switch):**
```
Speed: 1 Gbps (usually)
Carries: Tagged frames for MULTIPLE VLANs
Used for: Connecting switches together
Example: GigabitEthernet1/1, GigabitEthernet2/1 on core/agg

When device sends frame to trunk port:
- Frame keeps VLAN tag
- Receiving switch reads tag
- Forwards to correct VLAN
```

