## **FIREWALLS & SECURITY**

### **Why Two Firewalls?**

**Layered Security (Defense in Depth):**
```
Internet
   │
   ▼
[FW1: Outside-to-DMZ]
   │
   ├─ Stops most attacks here
   ├─ Blocks RFC1918 spoofing
   └─ Allows only HTTP/HTTPS
      │
      ▼
   [DMZ: Untrusted but filtered]
      │
      ▼
[FW2: DMZ-to-Inside]
   │
   ├─ More restrictive policies
   └─ Allows only necessary traffic
      │
      ▼
[Internal Network: Most trusted]
```

### **FW1 Configuration Explained:**

**Interfaces:**
```
GigabitEthernet1/1: 203.0.113.x (outside)
- Faces the internet
- Security level 0 (least trusted)
- Receives packets from unknown sources

GigabitEthernet1/2: 10.0.80.x (dmz)
- Faces the DMZ
- Security level 50 (semi-trusted)
- Forwards allowed packets to inside
```

**ACL Rules (FW1_IN):**

Rule 1: `deny ip any 10.0.0.0 255.255.0.0`
- Blocks any packet claiming to come from internal IPs
- Prevents spoofing (attacker pretending to be from inside)
- Why? Internet shouldn't have packets from 10.0.x.x!

Rule 2: `deny ip any 172.16.0.0 255.240.0.0` & `deny ip any 192.168.0.0 255.255.0.0`
- Blocks other RFC1918 private ranges
- Defense against spoofing

Rule 3: `permit tcp any any eq 80` & `permit tcp any any eq 443`
- Allows HTTP (port 80) and HTTPS (port 443)
- Customers need to access the bank website!

Rule 4: `permit tcp any any established`
- Allows return traffic from outbound connections
- If internal server made connection, allow responses

Rule 5: `deny ip any any`
- Implicit deny (default drop)
- Anything not explicitly allowed is blocked

### **FW2 Configuration Explained:**

**Interfaces:**
```
GigabitEthernet1/1: 10.0.80.x (dmz)
- Receives from FW1
- Security level 50

GigabitEthernet1/2: 10.0.50.x (inside)
- Faces internal network
- Security level 100 (most trusted)
```

**ACL Rules (FW2_DB) - Database Access Control:**

```
permit tcp 10.0.20.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.30.0 255.255.255.0 eq 3306
```
- Source: VLAN 20 (Web Servers)
- Destination: VLAN 30 (Database)
- Port: 3306 (MySQL)
- **Meaning:** "Web servers can connect to database"

```
permit tcp 10.0.50.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.30.0 255.255.255.0 eq 3306
```
- Source: VLAN 50 (Security - SOC, IDS, IPS)
- Destination: VLAN 30 (Database)
- Port: 3306 (MySQL)
- **Meaning:** "Security appliances can audit database"

```
deny ip any any
```
- Everything else is blocked
- Other VLANs CANNOT access database directly
- Example: VLAN 40 can't connect to VLAN 30

